## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-cloud IaC security policy gates using industry-standard tools (tfsec, Checkov); covers secret management, encryption, versioning, provider pinning, tagging, and backend security.

## Key Concepts
- Secrets in code and tfvars detection
- Cloud storage encryption and versioning
- Provider version pinning and backends
- Tagging and access controls across clouds
- CI integration and local verification for IaC gates

## Code Example
```javascript
// Failing: plaintext secret in Terraform
variable \"db_password\" { default = \"SuperSecret123\" }
resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"db\" {
  password = var.db_password
}

// Passing: secret retrieved from Secrets Manager
data \"aws_secretsmanager_secret_version\" \"db_pass\" {
  secret_id = \"arn:aws:secretsmanager:region:acct:secret:db-password\"
}
resource \"aws_db_instance\" \"db\" {
  password = data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.db_pass.secret_string
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit-test this gate locally? 
- How would you evolve checks for multi-cloud secret fetch patterns?

## Why This Is Asked
Designing runtime access control is critical for microservices in a ride-hailing platform. This tests integration of SPIFFE identities, policy as code (OPA), and Envoy ext_authz in a real-world, zero-trust setup.

## Key Concepts
- SPIFFE IDs and mTLS identity
- Open Policy Agent (Rego) for authz decisions
- Envoy ext_authz as a gatekeeper
- Service mesh zero-trust patterns
- CI test harness for policy validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal JavaScript test harness exercising OPA policy via REST API
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
async function test(spiffe_id, expected) {
  const input = { spiffe_id };
  const res = await fetch('http://opa.local/v1/data/authz/allow', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ input })
  });
  const json = await res.json();
  console.log(spiffe_id, json.result === expected);
}
(async () => { await test('spiffe://domain.org/finance/payment', true); await test('spiffe://domain.org/unknown', false); })();
```

## Why This Is Asked

Introduces a practical policy gate that enforces manifest hygiene early in CI, ensuring traceability and consistency across deployments.

## Key Concepts

- YAML manifest traversal and metadata fields
- Distinguishing annotations vs labels and required keys
- Lightweight Node.js validator using js-yaml
- Integrating validator into GitHub Actions as a PR gate

## Code Example

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const yaml = require('js-yaml');
function valid(manifest) {
  const m = yaml.load(fs.readFileSync(manifest, 'utf8'));
  const meta = m?.metadata || {};
  const ann = meta.annotations || {};
  const lab = meta.labels || {};
  return ann['app.kubernetes.io/managed-by'] === 'ci-tool' && lab.app && lab.version;
}
module.exports = { valid };
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend the validator to report which manifests fail and why?
- How would you adapt the workflow to run in parallel across multiple directories?

## Why This Is Asked
Addresses multi-cloud data governance, tenancy, and compliance through policy-as-code, ensuring data privacy, residency, and auditable CI validation in production-scale ML platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Data masking in logs/telemetry
- Per-tenant data residency and envelope encryption
- Tenant-aware RBAC across clouds
- Policy-as-code (OPA/Kyverno) + CI gates

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPII(logEntry) {
  const redacted = { ...logEntry };
  for (const k of Object.keys(redacted)) {
    if (k.toLowerCase().includes('ssn') || k.toLowerCase().includes('email')) {
      redacted[k] = 'REDACTED';
    }
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test fail-closed masking in CI across multiple tenants?
- What metrics would you surface to detect residency violations at runtime?
